{"meta":{"chapter_title":"AI Ethics and Ethobot 3.2","title":"Facial Recognition as Classroom Case","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting facial recognition as classroom case to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"04","section":"02","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_ethics_and_ethobot_3_2","cat531_supplement_facial_recognition_as_classroom_case","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/04/01"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_4_2_facial_recognition_as_classroom_case_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_4_2_facial_recognition_as_classroom_case_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_4_2_facial_recognition_as_classroom_case_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_4_2_facial_recognition_as_classroom_case_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":2,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Facial Recognition as Classroom Case\n\n![Facial Recognition as Classroom Case textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/04-02-facial-recognition-as-classroom-case.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nFacial recognition is the cleanest case study in the module because the trade it forces is so concrete. A vendor promises measurable time savings; independent testing shows the same system fails more often on darker-skinned faces; and the data it collects (a face template) is biometric and durable. A teacher who can reason through this one case can reason through almost any classroom AI proposal, because it contains all three pressures (efficiency, equity, privacy) in a single decision and refuses to let you satisfy all three at once.\n\nWhat makes this a judgment problem rather than a slogan is that \"facial recognition is biased, so no\" is not yet a defensible decision. It names one pole and skips the negotiation a colleague or principal will demand. The artifact you build, a tension map with a negotiated decision, exists precisely to show that you weighed the real benefit against the documented harm and the hidden data cost, and arrived somewhere you can defend with evidence.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Separate the three distinct pressures in an automated-attendance proposal: instructional time, demographic accuracy, and biometric data retention.\n- Read a vendor accuracy claim critically by asking for error rates broken out by subgroup, not a single aggregate number.\n- Build a tension map that attaches specific evidence to each pole rather than asserting a verdict.\n- State a negotiated decision with a re-entry condition, so the door is not simply closed but conditioned on measurable improvement.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA district pilots facial-recognition check-in to cut hallway tardies, and a teacher is asked to weigh in before the building-wide rollout. The pitch is appealing: ten minutes per period back for instruction. But the teacher knows two facts the pitch omits. The vendor's own documentation shows higher false-non-match rates for darker-skinned students, which means some students would be marked absent while standing in the doorway. And the face templates sit on a vendor server for two years. The decision is not \"is this cool\" but \"which value do I protect, and can I name a condition under which the trade would change.\"\n\n**Recent evidence.** This exact decision has already been made at state scale. The Lockport City School District in New York deployed the Aegis facial-recognition system (SN Technologies) for school security; an independent audit found the system's false-match rate for Black female faces was roughly 16 times that for white males. In 2020 New York enacted a statewide moratorium on biometric identifying technology in schools, and the state's Office of Information Technology Services report with NYSED concluded the risks of facial recognition in schools outweighed the benefits, recommending against it. The negotiated, evidence-conditioned decision this section teaches is the one the state reached.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA defensible facial-recognition decision treats the proposal as a set of competing values with evidence attached, not as a single feature to approve or reject. The mistake to avoid is collapsing the case to its most salient pole. \"It saves time\" hides the equity cost; \"it's biased\" hides the privacy cost and the real instructional benefit. A tension map keeps all the poles visible at once.\n\nThere are three distinct pressures, and they do not trade against each other on the same scale:\n\n- **Efficiency** is measured in recovered instructional minutes. Real, but bounded and substitutable (a manual roster also works).\n- **Equity** is measured in differential error rates across groups. A system that is 99 percent accurate overall can still be 90 percent accurate for one subgroup, and that subgroup absorbs the entire cost. This is documented, not hypothetical: NIST's large-scale vendor test found demographic differentials in false-non-match and false-positive rates across the large majority of the 189 algorithms tested (Grother, Ngan, & Hanaoka, 2019), and the Gender Shades audit found commercial systems most accurate for lighter-skinned men and least accurate for darker-skinned women, with error gaps up to roughly 34 percentage points (Buolamwini & Gebru, 2018).\n- **Privacy** is measured in what data is collected, where it lives, and for how long. A face template is biometric: unlike a password, a student cannot change their face if the data leaks. Specific law constrains this pole, not just prudence: Illinois BIPA requires informed written consent plus a public written retention-and-destruction schedule (destruction at purpose-completion or within 3 years, whichever comes first), and the FTC's 2025 COPPA amendments explicitly classify facial templates as children's personal information, bar indefinite retention, and require a written deletion-timeframe policy, as of 2026. The case's 24-month vendor retention already sits in tension with both.\n\nThe course anchors do specific work here. **Critical edtech studies** is what surfaces the equity pole, because aggregate accuracy claims are designed to make subgroup failure invisible; you have to ask for the breakdown. **[AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)** is what surfaces the privacy pole, naming the 24-month retention as a standing risk rather than a footnote. **Teacher professional judgment** is what produces the negotiated decision, the part no policy template can write for you: which value you protect now, and what evidence would justify revisiting.\n\n## Worked Example: Mapping the Facial-Recognition Tension\n\nA district piloted facial-recognition check-in to cut hallway tardies. A teacher must build a Design Tension Studio map naming the value conflict and a negotiated decision. Work it as numbered judgment calls.\n\nThe case facts:\n\n1. Stated benefit: attendance logging is automated, saving roughly 10 minutes per class period.\n2. Reported error: the vendor's accuracy drops for darker-skinned students, producing more false non-matches.\n3. Data fact: face templates are retained on a vendor server for 24 months.\n\nStep-by-step tension map:\n\n- Step 1. Name the poles. Pole A = instructional time recovered. Pole B = equitable, accurate treatment of every student.\n- Step 2. Attach evidence to each pole. Pole A evidence = the 10-minute saving. Pole B evidence = the documented accuracy gap by skin tone.\n- Step 3. Find the hidden third pole. Data retention (24 months on a vendor server) is a privacy tension the time-versus-equity framing hides.\n- Step 4. State the negotiated decision, not a winner. \"Use manual check-in for attendance; do not deploy facial recognition until the vendor shows parity across skin tones and 24-month retention is reduced.\"\n\nBefore (weak): \"Facial recognition is biased so we should not use it.\" This is a verdict with one pole and no negotiation.\n\nAfter (strong): \"The map weighs 10 minutes saved against a measured accuracy gap that falls hardest on darker-skinned students, plus 24-month vendor retention. Negotiated decision: keep manual check-in now; revisit only if the vendor demonstrates parity and shorter retention. Evidence for the equity pole = the vendor's own error-rate breakdown.\"\n\nThe strong map names both poles, attaches evidence, surfaces the privacy pole, and records a negotiated decision a reviewer can audit.\n\nYou are now in the adoption meeting and must make the call out loud, with a principal who wants a yes. Walk the decision.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The attendance-rollout decision\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"The principal pitches building-wide facial-recognition check-in: 10 minutes per period back for instruction. The vendor offers one accuracy number: 99 percent. What do you ask first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"\\\"Great, let us pilot it next week\\\"\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"You accepted a marketing aggregate. A 99 percent overall rate can hide a steep subgroup failure that some students absorb entirely.\"},{\"label\":\"\\\"Can you break the error rate out by student subgroup, including skin tone?\\\"\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Equity first. The aggregate is designed to make subgroup failure invisible, so you ask for the breakdown before anything else.\"},{\"label\":\"\\\"What matters most is the 10 minutes saved per period\\\"\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"You ranked efficiency on the same scale as equity. Saved minutes are recoverable by a manual roster; a misidentified student is not.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"Reconsider: what does a single 99 percent number conceal?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A subgroup failure rate borne by darker-skinned students\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Ask for the disaggregated rate first.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Trusting one number is the first misreading.\"},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"The vendor admits false-non-match rates are higher for darker-skinned students, and templates sit on its server for 24 months. What is your decision?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Adopt now; the bias is small and can be fixed later\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Adopting first and fixing later means the students who absorb the cost do so while you wait. The equity pole is unresolved.\"},{\"label\":\"Permanent ban: facial recognition is biased, so never\",\"next\":\"n6\",\"feedback\":\"A permanent ban is a one-pole verdict with no re-entry condition; it is not yet a negotiated decision.\"},{\"label\":\"Keep manual check-in now; condition adoption on documented parity and shorter retention\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"This is the negotiated decision: it protects the equity and privacy poles now and names the measurable change that would let the answer flip.\"}]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"Reconsider: are saved minutes the same kind of value as accurate, equitable treatment?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"No; minutes are substitutable, a misidentified student is not\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Run equity first, then privacy, then efficiency.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Ranking efficiency flat with equity is itself the error.\"},\"n5\":{\"prompt\":\"Re-anchor: who bears the cost while you ’fix it later’?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The darker-skinned students marked absent in the doorway\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"That is why the equity condition must be met before adoption, not after.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"n6\":{\"prompt\":\"Add the missing piece: what makes a decision negotiated rather than a verdict?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A re-entry condition the vendor could measurably meet\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Hold the door open on parity and retention, not a flat ban.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"end\":{\"prompt\":\"You held manual check-in and conditioned adoption on parity plus reduced retention.\",\"feedback\":\"A defensible biometric decision runs equity, then privacy, then efficiency, and ends in a negotiated decision with a measurable re-entry condition.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nFor any biometric classroom tool, run the poles in this order before you decide:\n\n1. **Equity first:** Ask for error rates by subgroup. If the vendor gives only an aggregate, treat the gap as unknown and therefore unacceptable until shown.\n2. **Privacy second:** Name what is collected, where it is stored, and the retention period. Biometric data raises the bar because it cannot be reissued, and because statute constrains it: face templates trigger FERPA's educational-record limits, Illinois BIPA's written-consent and retention-schedule requirements, and the 2025 COPPA amendments' no-indefinite-retention rule.\n3. **Efficiency last:** Quantify the benefit honestly, then ask whether a non-biometric method captures most of it.\n\nA negotiated decision must include a re-entry condition: the specific, measurable thing that would have to change for the answer to flip.\n\nPut the three pressures in the order the decision rule requires you to evaluate them.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Equity: ask for error rates broken out by subgroup\", \"Privacy: name what biometric data is collected, where it lives, and for how long\", \"Efficiency: quantify the benefit, then ask if a non-biometric method captures most of it\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A vendor says its lunch-line fingerprint reader is '98 percent accurate' and stores prints with a third-party processor. Which question does the decision rule make you ask before the efficiency benefit?\" explanation=\"Equity comes first: a flat 98 percent can hide that fingerprint readers fail more often for young children and worn prints, so you ask for the subgroup breakdown before you weigh the speed gain at all.\" options='[{\"text\":\"How many seconds per student does it save?\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"What is the failure rate broken out for young children and worn prints?\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Is the interface easy for staff to learn?\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Does it integrate with the existing payment system?\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is trusting a single accuracy number. \"99 percent accurate\" is a marketing average that can conceal a steep subgroup failure, and the students who absorb that failure are usually the ones with the least power to complain. The second is treating efficiency as a value on the same footing as equity. Saved minutes are recoverable by other means; a misidentified student or a leaked face template is not. Ranking them flat is itself the error.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the reasoning to a different biometric pitch. A vendor offers fingerprint scanning for lunch-line payment, sold as faster lines and no lost cards. Run the poles. Equity: fingerprint readers have known higher failure rates for young children and for worn or damaged prints, so some kids get held up daily, in front of peers, at the lunch line. Privacy: fingerprints are biometric and the contract stores them with a third-party payment processor. Efficiency: real, but a PIN pad captures most of the speed gain without biometric data. The negotiated decision writes itself once the poles are visible: adopt the PIN pad, decline the fingerprint reader, and name the re-entry condition (an on-device, no-third-party storage model with documented parity for young children). The same three-pole move that handled facial recognition handles this with no new theory.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nKeep a two-column note: each pole on the left, the exact evidence on the right (the subgroup error figure, the retention clause, the measured time saving). A strong reader of a facial-recognition case can state which pole the current design centers and which one absorbs the cost, and can name the one fact that would change the decision. If your note has a verdict but only one pole with evidence, you have an opinion, not a map.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong tension map shows both poles with evidence attached, surfaces the hidden privacy pole, and ends in a negotiated decision with a re-entry condition, not a winner-take-all verdict. Visual neatness is the last layer. If a pole has no evidence beside it, mark the gap and ask BigAL for the vendor-documentation question you should be asking rather than a general explanation of bias.\n\nA useful closing line for the artifact sounds like this: \"I centered the equity pole because the vendor's own breakdown shows the cost falls on darker-skinned students; I held the door open with a parity-and-retention condition rather than a permanent ban, so the decision stays evidence-driven.\"\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Explain why a negotiated decision with a re-entry condition ('revisit only if the vendor shows parity and shorter retention') is more defensible than either 'adopt it' or 'ban it permanently.'\" answer=\"'Adopt it' ignores the documented equity and privacy costs, and 'ban it permanently' is a one-pole verdict that pretends the efficiency benefit and any future vendor improvement do not exist. A negotiated decision protects the most-affected students now (manual check-in) while naming the specific, measurable change that would justify revisiting, so the decision is tied to evidence rather than to a fixed stance. A reviewer can audit it: they can check whether the parity and retention conditions were met, which is impossible to audit against a slogan.\"></self-explain>\n\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the case to a small rural school where the principal argues the demographic homogeneity makes the accuracy gap \"not really a problem here.\" That setting changes the equity calculus but does not erase it: the privacy pole is unchanged (templates still leave the building), and a single misidentified student in a small school is more, not less, visible. Working that version tests whether you treat equity as a checklist item that small numbers can wave away, or as a structural question about who bears the cost when a system fails.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/04-02-facial-recognition-as-classroom-case-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"04.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 04.02. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Concept Map\n\nThese are the concepts this section connects. Trace how reading a biometric case as competing values drives an evidence-based negotiated decision.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_ai_ethics_and_ethobot_3_2\", \"cat531_supplement_facial_recognition_as_classroom_case\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_facial_recognition_as_classroom_case\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision captures instructional purpose, equity or privacy boundary, support request, revision rationale, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- Perrotta, C., & Selwyn, N. (2019). Deep learning goes to school: Toward a relational understanding of AI in education. Learning, Media and Technology. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n- Grother, P., Ngan, M., & Hanaoka, K. (2019). Face Recognition Vendor Test (FRVT) Part 3: Demographic Effects (NIST IR 8280). National Institute of Standards and Technology. https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.IR.8280\n- Buolamwini, J., & Gebru, T. (2018). Gender Shades: Intersectional accuracy disparities in commercial gender classification. *Proceedings of Machine Learning Research, 81*, 77-91. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v81/buolamwini18a.html\n- Federal Trade Commission (2025). Children's Online Privacy Protection Rule, Final Amendments. *Federal Register* (Apr. 22, 2025). https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/04/22/2025-05904/childrens-online-privacy-protection-rule\n- Illinois Biometric Information Privacy Act, 740 ILCS 14. https://www.ilga.gov/legislation/ilcs/ilcs3.asp?ActID=3004\n- New York statewide moratorium on biometric technology in schools and the Lockport/Aegis case. https://www.biometricupdate.com/202007/facial-biometrics-in-new-york-state-schools-blocked-as-legislature-passes-2-year-moratorium\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_2_facial_recognition_as_classroom_case_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_facial_recognition_as_classroom_case","stem":"A vendor reports that its classroom facial-recognition check-in is 98 percent accurate overall. What should a teacher check before treating that number as evidence the tool is fair?","options":["Whether 98 percent is higher than the previous vendor's number.","Whether accuracy is broken down by student subgroup, since an average can hide a larger error rate for darker-skinned students.","Whether the 2 percent error happens in the morning or afternoon.","Whether the school can afford the licensing fee."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Overall accuracy can mask subgroup error. The equity tension lives in the breakdown, not the average.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_2_accuracy_equals_fairness","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_facial_recognition_as_classroom_case","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_2_facial_recognition_as_classroom_case_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_facial_recognition_as_classroom_case","stem":"A student submits a tension map that reads only: 'Facial recognition is biased, so the school should ban it.' What is the most useful revision note?","options":["Add a citation to make the claim sound more academic.","Name the opposing pole and its evidence (time saved at check-in) so the map shows a negotiated decision rather than a single verdict.","Make the map more colorful so it reads as finished.","Replace 'biased' with a stronger word."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A tension map needs both poles with evidence; a one-pole verdict is not yet a negotiated decision.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_2_verdict_not_negotiation","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_facial_recognition_as_classroom_case","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_2_facial_recognition_as_classroom_case_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_facial_recognition_as_classroom_case","stem":"A teacher maps the case purely as 'instructional time saved versus equity.' Which question most clearly reveals a value the map is hiding?","options":["How many minutes per period are actually saved?","Where are the students' face templates stored, by whom, and for how long?","Which teacher proposed the pilot first?","Does the district like the vendor's customer service?"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Biometric data retention is a third, privacy pole the time-versus-equity framing hides.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_2_ignoring_data_retention","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_facial_recognition_as_classroom_case","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_2_facial_recognition_as_classroom_case_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"Which negotiated decision is most defensible given a documented subgroup accuracy gap and 24-month vendor retention of face templates?","options":["Deploy the system district-wide because it saves 10 minutes per period.","Keep manual check-in now and revisit only if the vendor shows parity across skin tones and reduces retention.","Ban all attendance technology permanently.","Let each teacher decide privately without recording the reasoning."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The strong decision names conditions for change tied to the evidence, so a reviewer can audit it.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_2_verdict_not_negotiation","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","cat531_supplement_facial_recognition_as_classroom_case"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_2_accuracy_equals_fairness","pattern":"high_accuracy_means_fair","description":"Assuming a facial-recognition tool that is accurate on average is therefore fair to every student group","trigger":"learner cites an overall accuracy number and concludes the tool is equitable","feedback":"Average accuracy hides subgroup error. Check the breakdown by skin tone or group; a gap that falls on one group is an equity tension, not a rounding error.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_2_verdict_not_negotiation","pattern":"single_verdict_instead_of_tension_map","description":"Producing a yes-or-no verdict on facial recognition instead of mapping the competing values and a negotiated decision","trigger":"learner writes 'it is biased, ban it' or 'it saves time, use it' with one pole only","feedback":"A tension map names both poles with evidence and records a negotiated decision. Replace the verdict with the conflict and the conditions under which the decision could change.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_2_ignoring_data_retention","pattern":"time_versus_equity_hides_privacy","description":"Framing the case only as instructional time versus equity and missing the data-retention privacy tension","trigger":"learner maps only two poles and never asks where face templates are stored or for how long","feedback":"Surface the hidden privacy pole. Ask where biometric templates are kept, by whom, and for how long, then add it to the tension map.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"56847b8a12ef97999407a4d2af2cf03742d70439f964d8725b2bce537dd33a76","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}